> **This is a standalone design system file. Add it as `.cursorrules-design`
> alongside all four phase files. It governs EVERY visual and interaction
> decision across all phases. When any phase rule conflicts with this file,
> this file wins on UI/UX matters.**

> **Cursor: read this file in full before touching a single component, color,
> or layout. Do not generate generic "health app blue" defaults. Every choice
> here is intentional, specific to this product, and has a reason behind it.**

---

## 0. THE PSYCHOLOGY THIS APP MUST HANDLE

Before any design decision, understand who is using this app and what emotional
state they're in. This is not a fitness tracker or a meditation app. A person
using LifeShield is:

- **Possibly anxious** — they're thinking about breast cancer risk. Even if they
  feel calm, the topic carries background dread.
- **Potentially vulnerable** — health information makes people feel exposed and
  judged. They don't want to feel like a patient.
- **Seeking control** — the reason they're here is to feel empowered, not scared.
  The app must give them a sense of agency and progress, not overwhelm.
- **Likely skeptical** — they don't fully trust apps with their health data yet.
  The design must signal safety, credibility, and clinical rigour without
  feeling cold or institutional.
- **Mobile-first, time-limited** — they're probably doing this in 10-minute
  windows, on their phone, in a normal life setting. Not at a desk, not
  unhurried.

**What this means for every design decision:**

- Calm > clinical. Warm > sterile. Empowered > scared.
- Progress must always be visible. A person who can see how far they've come
  and how far remains will not abandon the questionnaire.
- Never lead with risk or fear. Lead with action and understanding.
- Reduce cognitive load at every step. One question, one action, one decision
  at a time. Never a wall of text or a wall of form fields.
- Celebrate completion. Every finished section deserves a micro-moment of
  acknowledgement — it builds momentum.
- Clinical credibility must be felt, not shouted. Achieved through precision
  typography, measured spacing, and restrained colour — not through stethoscope
  icons and blue crosses.

