## 3. TYPOGRAPHY

**Font stack:**
```
Display/Headings: 'DM Serif Display'   — emotional weight, human, precise
Body/UI:          'Inter'              — clean, legible at small sizes, universal
Mono (numbers):   'DM Mono'            — for lab values, percentages, scores
```

Install: `npx expo install @expo-google-fonts/dm-serif-display @expo-google-fonts/inter @expo-google-fonts/dm-mono`

**Why these:**
DM Serif Display has a warmth and elegance that says "considered, human, premium"
without feeling old-fashioned. Paired with Inter (the gold standard for UI
legibility on screen), the combination reads: sophisticated but approachable.
DM Mono keeps numbers clinical-precise without switching the whole interface into
code mode.

**Type scale (rem-equivalent, use these exact sizes):**
```
Display:    32sp  DM Serif Display  bold   — screen titles, welcome moments
H1:         26sp  DM Serif Display  normal — section headers
H2:         20sp  Inter             600    — card titles, group headers
H3:         17sp  Inter             600    — list item titles
Body:       15sp  Inter             400    — all body text
Body-sm:    13sp  Inter             400    — secondary text, captions
Label:      12sp  Inter             500    — field labels, tags
Medical:    13sp  DM Mono           400    — lab values, clinical terms
Micro:      11sp  Inter             400    — timestamps, metadata
```

**Line heights:**
- Display/H1: 1.2 (tight, dramatic)
- H2/H3: 1.3
- Body: 1.6 (generous — reading health info needs breathing room)
- Body-sm: 1.5

**Letter spacing:**
- Display/H1: -0.5px (optically tighter for large serif)
- Body and below: 0 (never track out body text — it reads clinical and cold)
- Labels: +0.2px (subtle, helps legibility at tiny sizes)
- NEVER use ALL CAPS for labels — it reads as institutional and cold

