## 7. SCREEN-BY-SCREEN UX RULES

**Every screen must have:**
1. One primary action (one PrimaryButton or a clear next step)
2. A clear heading that states what this screen is for
3. Visible progress if inside the questionnaire or onboarding
4. An obvious back path (never trap the user)
5. An empty/loading state that is designed, not a spinner alone

### Splash
- DM Serif Display 32sp "LifeShield" centred, deep teal on cream
- Tagline 15sp Inter 400 slate, below
- No animation beyond a clean fade-in (2 frames, not a production sequence)
- Auto-advance to auth after 1.8 seconds if already logged in

### Onboarding (3 screens max)
- One key idea per screen, illustrated with a simple Feather-icon composition
- Horizontal swipe between screens, dot indicators
- "Skip" TextButton top-right from screen 2 onward
- Final screen CTA: "Get started" PrimaryButton

### Auth (Register/Login)
- Single column layout, 20px horizontal padding
- Email first, password below, generous spacing
- "Show password" eye icon in field
- Error messages inline below the field, coral, 13sp
- "Forgot password" TextButton below password on login
- Social/Google login: NOT included (health data + Google = trust concern)

### Triage (most important screen in the app)
- Cream background — calm, not alarming
- DM Serif Display H1: "Before we begin" — sets an intentional, measured tone
- Subheading: "We need to ask one important question to make sure this service
  is right for you right now."
- Three RadioGroup cards: "I have breast pain" / "I have breast discomfort" /
  "I've noticed a lump"  (each with an "Yes" / "No" inner toggle)
- Space between the options and the CTA is generous — no accidental taps
- PrimaryButton only enabled when all three are answered

### Questionnaire sections
- QuestionnaireStepper pinned at top (always visible, never scrolls away)
- One section per screen — no scrolling through multiple topics at once
- Field labels always above the input, never inside as placeholder-only
- "Why do we ask this?" info icon on any question that might feel intrusive.
  Tapping opens a small bottom sheet with a one-paragraph plain explanation.
- "Save & Continue" PrimaryButton pinned at bottom (sticky footer) — always
  reachable without scrolling
- Keyboard-aware: screen scrolls up when keyboard appears, CTA stays visible
- After save: SectionCompleteCard for 1.5s, then auto-advance

### Results dashboard
- Status chips lead — value in the detail
- Group by status: "Worth watching" first (action-oriented), then "Within range"
- Never sort by alphabetical or by test tier (users don't think in tiers)
- No red anywhere on this screen unless a result is critical — most users will
  see amber at worst, and the design must not pre-alarm them

### Personalised plan
- "Pending review" banner top — sage-coloured (not amber/warning) because
  pending review is a GOOD thing (a clinician is looking after you)
- Group by: Daily habits / Food & drink / Supplements / Follow-up tests /
  See a specialist. Never by clinical category.
- Each card: action title first, reason second, clinical basis collapsed
- Empty state if no items: "Your practitioner is reviewing your results.
  We'll notify you when your plan is ready." (never just a spinner)

### Store
- "Recommended for you" section at top — maximum 5 items, filtered to this
  user's triggers. Headline: "Based on your results" not "Recommended products"
- ProductCard grid (2 columns on phone) for the full catalog
- Safety states (blocked/flagged) styled as information, not errors

### Pathway B exit
- NEVER a modal. Full screen. This deserves full attention.
- Calm, warm, directive. Not a red screen of doom.
- See Section 5.5 PathwayBBanner for exact spec

